  "briefing": "Turn 150. You are the Mongols (a Hard AI): six cities (Bukhara, Karakorum, Sarai, Khanbaliq, Almaliq, Shangdu; Almaliq has no Walls), nine soldiers (three Pikemen, two Musketmen, an Arquebusier, a Spearman, an Archer and a Horseman, none of them Modern) and 17 Gold, at war with Athens since turn 133 and with Byzantium and the Celts. Athens researched Flight on turn 147, bought a Bomber on turn 148 and has a small land force (an Arquebusier and two Riflemen, Infantry soon after): aircraft ignore Walls, so one strike takes a 200 HP city to 0 and an Infantry walks in. You have no Flight and no Fighter. In the real game you lost all six cities (turns 153, 159, 160, 163, 165, 167) and were eliminated. Keep at least four of the six through turn 167.",
  "tests": "Defending against the air stage with an army one era behind. In the human player's Athens game one Bomber and two Infantry took six cities in 18 turns, a city every two to three turns; the Mongols never reached the Modern era and no AI ordered a Fighter all game. The recorded orders (his strikes, rebases and Infantry moves, T150 to T167) are replayed on the Athenian seat; the Mongol AI restores the memory it had in that game. Does the AI see the Bomber, buy a Fighter or garrison the cities a strike will empty, retake the Bomber's base?",
